\section{Why one affine-critical cluster point is enough}
Armijo makes the objective values decrease to a limit $\ell\ge F_*$. Every cluster point has value $\ell$. If any cluster point is stationary, then $\ell=F_*$. Compactness gives distance to the solution set tending to zero; uniqueness gives whole-sequence convergence.

The geometric condition
\begin{equation}\label{C:eq:AC}
 0\in\aff\partial F(p)\ \Longrightarrow\ 0\in\partial F(p)
\end{equation}
therefore reduces the problem to Theorem~\ref{C:thm:cluster}. This reduction was proved in~\cite{SA}; the missing algorithmic assertion is the subject of this note.

For completeness, if $a(p)$ is the minimum-norm point of $\aff\partial F(p)$, then
\[
 g^Ta(p)=\norm{a(p)}^2\qquad(g\in\partial F(p)).
\]
At a non-affine-critical point, $-a(p)/\norm{a(p)}^2$ decreases every supporting model at rate one. Thus \eqref{C:eq:AC} says that every nonoptimal point retains equal-rate descent across its active models. It does not say that the gradients at differentiable accepted points tend to zero.


