\section{Vanishing steps without an assumed iterate limit}
Put
\[
 q_k=g_k^TH_kg_k,\quad d_k=-g_k^Ts_k=\alpha_kq_k,\quad
 \Delta_k=F(x_k)-F(x_{k+1}),\quad c=1-c_2>0.
\]
Convexity and \eqref{C:eq:AW} give
\begin{equation}\label{C:eq:eligible}
 d_k>0,\qquad c_1d_k\le\Delta_k\le d_k,\qquad t_k\ge c d_k.
\end{equation}
In particular $\sum_k d_k<\infty$. A finite convex function has bounded subgradients on a compact set: compare supporting planes at $x\in K$ with values on a fixed enlarged compact neighborhood. Hence the accepted gradients are bounded, and Module~\ref{M:chapter} applies.

\begin{lemma}[Strict contours force vanishing steps]\label{C:lem:steps}
If $F(x_k)\downarrow\ell>F_*$ and $K_\ell$ is strictly convex, then $s_k\to0$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Otherwise compactness gives a subsequence with $x_k\to x$ and $x_{k+1}\to z\ne x$. For every $0\le\theta\le1$, the supporting inequality at the accepted point $x_k$ and convexity give
\[
 F(x_k)-\theta d_k
 \le F((1-\theta)x_k+\theta x_{k+1})
 \le(1-\theta)F(x_k)+\theta F(x_{k+1}).
\]
Since $d_k\to0$ and both endpoint values tend to $\ell$, continuity shows that $F=\ell$ on $[x,z]$. A level point of a continuous convex function lies on the boundary of its sublevel whenever some point has strictly smaller value; here a minimizer has value $F_*<\ell$. Thus $[x,z]$ is a nontrivial boundary segment of $K_\ell$, contradicting strict convexity. This argument does not require a convergent subsequence of gradients.
\end{proof}

We will argue by contradiction that the compact cluster set $\Gamma$ contains no affine-critical point. Then $\ell>F_*$, every point of $\Gamma$ satisfies the regular-switching hypothesis, and Lemma~\ref{C:lem:steps} applies. This does not assume that $\Gamma$ is a singleton, or even use its connectedness.


